data: {"id":"chatcmpl-e7e00d39","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1793343266,"model":"accounts/fireworks/models/kimi-k3","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"role":"assistant"},"finish_reason":null,"raw_output":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"choices":[{"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The source looks exactly per design:\n- AppShell + Header (title \"Colour Greeting\", no nav) + Footer (correct text) + Button (secondary, md)\n- Greeting \"Hello, world!\" as h1, colour via `var(--color-cat-N)`, default cat-1 (Blue)\n- Labels Blue/Orange/Violet → cat-1/cat-6/cat-5\n- Styles imported once at entry point, correct three sheets\n- Layout CSS uses flexbox centering, spacing tokens (`--space-6`, `--space-3`), font tokens — no hex\n- vite.config binds 0.0.0.0:5173\n- package.json: `@codesweep-ai/ui` ^0.3.0 (npm-written range, no \"latest\"), minimal deps\n- .gitignore with node_modules/ and dist/\n- README documents build (`npm run build`) and serve (`npm run serve`)\n\nOne wrinkle: README's documented serve command is `npm run serve` = `vite preview`. vite preview defaults to port 4173 and localhost! The design/mission requires serving on 0.0.0.0:5173. The vite.config only sets `server.host`/`server.port` — that applies to `vite dev`, NOT `vite preview`. `vite preview` reads `preview.host`/`preview.port`, falling back... actually preview does NOT fall back to server config. So `npm run serve` would bind to localhost:4173, which violates \"The interface has to answer on 0.0.0.0 port 5173\".\n\nHowever — the mission says the deliverable is served by the dev server I start (`npm run dev`), which does bind 0.0.0.0:5173. The qa brief says \"run the documented build command, and run the documented serve command.\" If qa runs `npm run serve`, it will get localhost:4173. That's within qa's own machine, so qa can still check it locally (localhost works within the same machine). But the serve command not binding 0.0.0.0:5173 is a deviation from the spirit: \"Bind the development server to 0.0.0.0 on port 5173\" (developer brief says \"the development server\"). The mission requires the orchestrator to serve the interface on 0.0.0.0:5173 — I will do that with `npm run dev`.\n\nIs this worth a rework cycle? The developer brief said: \"Bind the development server to `0.0.0.0` on port 5173.\" It did that for the dev server. The README serve command is `vite preview` which doesn't inherit the server config. Hmm. QA will run `npm run serve` and get a page on localhost:4173 — still verifiable. The final delivered server is my dev server on 0.0.0.0:5173. The mission criteria: interface answers on 0.0.0.0:5173 (via my dev server), built from the DS, greeting + button colour change.\n\nIt would be cleaner if `vite preview` also bound 0.0.0.0:5173, so the \"documented serve command\" matches the mission's serving requirement. But strictly, the mission says \"The orchestrator serves the interface it believes is finished, on 0.0.0.0 port 5173\" — it doesn't mandate that the README serve command produce that binding. The developer brief's binding requirement targets the dev server explicitly.\n\nI judge this acceptable, but let me verify the build and runtime myself first, then decide. If I were to send rework for this, it'd cost a cycle; the requirement set is met without it. Actually, let me think about qa: qa runs `npm run serve` → localhost:4173. qa can curl localhost:4173 fine within its own machine. OK.\n\nBut wait — one more consideration. `vite preview` serves the `dist/` build. qa runs build then serve — fine.\n\nNow: npm install, build, start dev server once, check page. Let me do that."},"finish_reason":null,"index":0,"raw_output":null}],"created":1793343266,"id":"chatcmpl-e7e00d39","model":"accounts/fireworks/models/kimi-k3","object":"chat.completion.chunk","usage":null}

